REALROM sample.note c043 [NesBankingAnalyzer] Bank value is RESTORED here, not resolved: this call's helper FUN_dca8 is a proven no-argument restore entry -- its own prologue reloads the bank directly from 0065, a writable RAM cell nothing between that load and the switch touches, and commits it unchanged. The bank after this call is therefore whatever was last saved at 0065, not a fresh value this analyzer failed to pin down -- a property of the game, not a gap in analysis.
REALROM sample.note dcaa [NesBankingAnalyzer] Bank value is supplied at each ENTRY: this code is reached from several entries into the helper, and on every path the value is fixed at the entry -- either the caller's argument, or a bank the entry's own prologue restores from RAM -- so the bank cannot be known from inside the helper body. Each entry's CALL SITES are judged separately; see their own annotations.
REALROM sample.note dcba [NesBankingAnalyzer] Bank value is supplied at each ENTRY: this code is reached from several entries into the helper, and on every path the value is fixed at the entry -- either the caller's argument, or a bank the entry's own prologue restores from RAM -- so the bank cannot be known from inside the helper body. Each entry's CALL SITES are judged separately; see their own annotations.
REALROM sample.note dcc3 [NesBankingAnalyzer] Bank value is this helper's ARGUMENT: the backward scan reached the helper's entry, where the register holds whatever the caller supplied, so the bank cannot be known from inside the helper body. Each CALL SITE is resolved separately; see the call sites' own annotations.
REALROM sample.note dcd3 [NesBankingAnalyzer] Bank value is this helper's ARGUMENT: the backward scan reached the helper's entry, where the register holds whatever the caller supplied, so the bank cannot be known from inside the helper body. Each CALL SITE is resolved separately; see the call sites' own annotations.
